WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.633
LangGraph.

2
00:00:01.933 --> 00:00:07.067
What if the agent loop you wrote by hand became a graph you could pause, save, and resume?

3
00:00:08.267 --> 00:00:10.933
LangGraph models an agent as a state graph.

4
00:00:11.233 --> 00:00:14.633
Nodes do the work, like calling the model or running tools.

5
00:00:14.933 --> 00:00:17.901
Edges route between them, and can branch or loop.

6
00:00:17.901 --> 00:00:19.900
All of it shares one typed state.

7
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:22.167
Here is the graph.

8
00:00:22.467 --> 00:00:26.133
We start at the agent node, which decides: answer, or act?

9
00:00:26.433 --> 00:00:30.033
If it wants a tool, a conditional edge sends it to the tools node.

10
00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:32.454
The result goes back to the agent.

11
00:00:32.454 --> 00:00:34.700
That cycle is the agent loop itself.

12
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.467
When the agent has an answer, the graph ends.

13
00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:39.867
Here it is in code.

14
00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:43.300
We add two nodes, one for the model and one for tools.

15
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.667
A conditional edge picks the next step based on state.

16
00:00:46.967 --> 00:00:49.567
This edge from tools back to agent is the loop.

17
00:00:49.867 --> 00:00:55.664
And compiling with a Postgres checkpointer saves the full state after every step, durably.

18
00:00:55.664 --> 00:00:58.433
An in-memory saver would vanish on restart.

19
00:00:59.633 --> 00:01:02.167
Two features make this the production standard.

20
00:01:02.467 --> 00:01:08.467
Checkpointers persist state, so you get crash recovery, time travel debugging, and resumable runs.

21
00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:12.733
Interrupts pause the graph at a node and wait for a human to approve.

22
00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:15.267
Here's where it pays off.

23
00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:20.167
A data source stalls a nine step run at step six, and the process restarts.

24
00:01:20.467 --> 00:01:30.033
Because the checkpoints live in Postgres, you invoke again with no new input and the same thread id, and it resumes instead of paying for six steps again.

25
00:01:30.333 --> 00:01:35.700
And an interrupt before the tools node lets a human approve hours later, with state intact.

26
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:37.732
Let's recap.

27
00:01:37.732 --> 00:01:39.533
An agent is a state graph.

28
00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:42.767
A conditional edge decides: answer or act.

29
00:01:43.067 --> 00:01:45.333
The cycle back to the agent is the loop.

30
00:01:45.633 --> 00:01:49.075
Checkpoints and interrupts make it production ready.

31
00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:50.133
Next up: CrewAI.
